Z||000000||87ECO5||06-22-2000||01:41 PM||||I ran a search but couldn't find where this had been discussed before.  I apologize if it's a duplicate thread.<P>I like to run Shell Premium through my '87 because it reduces the pinging produced by my 2.5 but, at $2.20/gallon, it's a little tough on the wallet.<P>I've heard it's just as effective to use low octane fuel with an octane boost additive mixed in.  Is this true?<p>[This message has been edited by 87ECO5 (edited 06-22-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||lennyb4||06-22-2000||02:10 PM||||Yep sure is. We discussed this before i think the post was called " Fuel additives" or something like that. It also has the mixture formulas too  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Since gas went AWOL i've been using the cheap stuff and adding an additive called "101 Octane Boost" (get the one for off road use only, it will give you an extra 2 - 4 points over the regular street version).||||reg||1
Z||000002||87ECO5||06-22-2000||02:33 PM||||I've heard that those high octane additives can burn the valves.  Is this true?||||reg||5
Z||000003||lennyb4||06-22-2000||03:00 PM||||You got me there. But if i put in 1 can to a full tank of cheap stuff ( 87 octane ) i estimate i should be running around 98 to 100 octane. The can says to mix one can per thirty gallons for a mixture of 3.0 - 5.0 octane increase. So i cut the difference and thats how i got my rough octane amount. Ive been doing this for around two weeks and runs smoother then ever.||||reg||1
Z||000004||87ECO5||06-22-2000||03:18 PM||||STP Gas Treatment promises to remove dirt and water from the fuel system as well as clean the fuel system components.<P>Two more questions:  (a) does this stuff really work and (b) does it hurt to run it <I>AND</I> the octane boost additive at the same time?<P>Thanks for your help, lennyb4.  Hope to see you at 2M4 Dale's place in August!||||reg||5
Z||000005||Raydar||06-22-2000||03:20 PM||||My 88 Duck usta ping terribly, at steady cruise speed. Even with the high octane stuff. Found out the EGR valve was bad. (Flunked smog.) Replaced the EGR valve. Passed smog and no longer pings, even with the rotgut gas.<BR>These things were designed to run on regular. Unless they have a lot of carbon built up, they still should.  <BR>Just my $.02.<BR>BTW... My V-6 never pinged at all, even when the EGR was FUBAR.<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 06-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||lennyb4||06-22-2000||03:33 PM||||My dad swears by that stuff always has and he probally always will. Ive personnally never used it. But hes never had a problem with it and if you asked him he'd tell ya to use it in a heartbeat. As for the pinging what your probally experianceing is pre-detenation and higher octane will reduce or eliminate it. As for mixing them..ummmm..i really dont know, might try reading the labels for a chemical reaction warning. If theres nothing on them then its probally safe to do it. Oh hell yaaa. Im going to norwalk not to sure if ill be able to make it out early enough to meet at dales but i will deffinately see you guys there..<P>** What time are you guys leaving from dales to go to norwalk? If its not to early i might be able to meet you all there.||||reg||1
Z||000007||87ECO5||06-22-2000||03:47 PM||||I'm probably approaching topics that have already been discussed but ...<P>What's the best way to get rid of carbon and keep it gone?||||reg||5
Z||000008||Patrick||06-22-2000||03:53 PM||||<BR>lennyb4, are you really saving any money by buying regular gas and using an octane booster as opposed to simply buying higher octane gas?||||reg||5
Z||000009||lennyb4||06-22-2000||04:04 PM||||Well around my area 87 octane is averaging $1.67 gal while premium is averaging $2.05 gal. and for racing fuel its averaging out around $4.00 gal.I can get the octane boost for around $3.50 and when added to 87 octane i end up with 98 - 100 octane (racing octane is only 103 octane). So actually i think it works out pretty well. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||deceler8||06-22-2000||04:09 PM||||Going to a 180 thermostat might allow you to use regular, unless you've really advanced the timing.<P>Hell, you might save enough the first tank to pay for the 'stat.||||reg||1
Z||000011||olympic||06-22-2000||05:15 PM||||87EC05,<BR>  I've cleaned the carbon out of several carburated cars bu simply spraying hot water from a spray bottle down the carb.<BR>   Drive the car a few miles so the engine is hot and then have someone keep it revved up while you spray hot water down the carb.  The water goes into the cylinders and explodes into steam, steam cleaning them.  Works great.  I've never done this to a F/I car so I'm not sure if it would affect the computer or anything.  <P>   Oh yeah, dont forget to change the oil afterwards.<p>[This message has been edited by olympic (edited 06-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||88formula||06-23-2000||05:34 PM||||I know someone who hydro-locked their engine doing that and bent one connecting rod. I know that because I rebuilt the engine for him.||||reg||1
Z||000013||olympic||06-24-2000||03:35 AM||||  Thats why I use a small spray bottle instead of just pouring water down the carb.  I give it a few squirts then let the engine recover and then do it again and again.<BR>  As for cleaning sludge out of the crank case,  I've heard of people draining their oil and then filling their crank case with a litre of oil and 4 litres of diesel fuel.  Run the engine for a while and drain the engine.  Suppose to work pretty good.  ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Patrick||06-24-2000||04:24 AM||||<BR>88, now you've scared me! I've done a variation of the procedure that olympic has described a couple of times on my 2.5L Fiero. This variation was told to me by the service manager of a Pontiac dealership and it goes something like this-<P>There are two tubes that go to the fuel vapor canister. Disconnect the one that has the highest vacuum from the base of the TBI and attach another tube to this TBI fitting. Keep the engine running at 3000 RPM while the other end of the tube is immersed in a bucket half filled with water. (I also put a fitting on the end of the immersed tube that has a very small opening.) Keep the engine running in this manner until the bucket is empty.<P>Now I can't really say if this has made any difference in the way my engine runs, but it's an interesting way to empty half a bucket of water. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">My biggest concern was that part of the underside of the car was going to catch fire from the glowing red hot catalytic converter. The engine has to work pretty hard to keep the revs up to 3000 while it's sucking in the water. Keep in mind that normally the car would be moving (at this RPM and work load) and there would be air rushing past to help cool everything down. If nothing else, at least I know that my cat is/was functioning. <BR>||||reg||4
Z||000015||KnightRyder31||06-24-2000||12:09 PM||||hmmm....I'm wondering, what if you put in 101 oct. booster(off road), and then filled your tank up with high oct. gas? Would that be like racing fuel??? Or would it be bad for your engine? Also, one of my friends stole some jet fuel from an airport, and mixed it with reg. gas, he said it was like adding nos shots...I thought he was full of crap, because I was thinking it would kill the engine, would it???||||reg||1
Z||000016||BBBAD GT||06-24-2000||03:53 PM||||I don't want to start anything, but I remember reading on different BBS's that those octane boosters, while pretty potient, doesn't add the actual "points" it says it does.  It has to do with the calculated number the gas companies use to determine the rating I think.  <P>I don't believe if you buy 93 octane gas, put in the black bottle of octane boost saying it would increase it say 4-7 points, that you would have 100 octane gas.  <P>I'm not a chemist, but like I said, I've read posts on guys who are and was explaining how it not just adding the points the bottles say.  I could be wrong tho, anybody heard this also?<P>Personally, I use the black bottle (always have one ready in the car) when I go to our local strip, because I only run pump gas there.  If I go to a big race, 104+ racegas  is the only gas I put in it.  It allows me to run more timing on my blown 5.0, which we all know equals power.  <P>BTW, more octane makes the fuel burn slower (I believe it stableizes it better).  Putting more octane in a car that doesn't need it, will only slow it down.  You should see the guys who just scratch their head after spending $4/gal on racegas at the strip in their bone stock car and run slower.  Its funny.<P>Yup, found a post about this issue, and from what I've read, an octane point is a tenth of an octane.  So if the bottle says it raises it 7 octane points, then your 93 octane is now 93.7.  Not much for the price of the stuff.  I'll dig some more for more 'technical' stuff.....<BR><p>[This message has been edited by BBBAD GT (edited 06-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||hugh||06-24-2000||09:39 PM||||As far as using jet fuel,your friend was either very imaginative ,or had a diesel in his car.Jet fuel is only kerosene.I have put tuolene from a paint supply house in my GN in the past.I forget the ratio to use(I can probably find it),but it cleans the injectors and raises the octane a few points.This was done by more than a few people that were running Grand Nationals. ||||reg||1
Z||000018||fierospeeder||06-25-2000||03:01 AM||||jet fuel, and most prop planes run off a higher octane fuel<P>if you go to a gas station, there is a formula for octane  its something like r+m/2<BR>unless someone knows it for sure||||reg||1
